<p>President Trump left Beijing on Friday after a state visit that included two days of talks with Chinese President Xi Jinping. Trump described the trip as a success, saying the governments had reached trade understandings and resolved several disputes. Xi called the visit a “milestone,” and Chinese officials said the sides reached “common understandings” on economic ties and strategic stability. Several accounts, however, said few concrete agreements had been confirmed by the summit’s end. Boeing later confirmed China had committed to an initial purchase of 200 aircraft, after Trump said the order could eventually reach 750 planes. The talks covered Taiwan, trade, technology and the conflict involving Iran, but Washington and Beijing emphasized different priorities in their public statements. Trump said he made no commitment to Xi on Taiwan and had not decided whether to proceed with a $14 billion arms package for Taipei. Chinese accounts said Xi warned that mishandling Taiwan could put U.S.-China relations in an “extremely dangerous” position. Trump also said Xi opposed Iran obtaining nuclear weapons, supported reopening the Strait of Hormuz and pledged not to send military equipment to Tehran. China’s Foreign Ministry did not publicly confirm those details, instead urging a lasting ceasefire, negotiations and the reopening of shipping lanes.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/47ilcubnncomc3dtsKtgZkUkcQoTmxeSGKbwda8VGK87it057iw0ehn6tUc4vGngzAA2vkkvI72cib9BwCp_7DiJwPwcRmxVbe6mweVx8iYr' alt='President Donald Trump talks with China&#x27;s President Xi Jinping at the Zhongnanhai leadership compound in Beijing on May 15, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Strategic framing: Xi invoked the “Thucydides Trap,” a warning about rivalry between a rising power and an incumbent one, before turning to Taiwan risks.</li><li>Trade machinery: Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi said both sides agreed to continue implementing existing trade agreements and establish trade and investment councils.</li><li>Press tensions: U.S. and Chinese press and security teams clashed behind the scenes despite cordial public meetings between Trump and Xi.</li></ul><blockquote>We’ve made some fantastic trade deals, great for both countries - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Trump: Trump said Xi, not Washington, raised Taiwan arms sales and said hearing Xi out did not amount to a change in U.S. policy. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/05/15/trump-taiwan-arm-sales-00923280'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Chinese Foreign Ministry: When asked about Trump’s aircraft-order claim, spokesman Guo Jiakun did not confirm it and said only that U.S.-China economic relations are mutually beneficial and that both sides should implement the leaders’ consensus. (<a href='https://news.cnyes.com/news/id/6460274'>Cnyes</a>)</li><li>Taiwanese officials: Taipei said it would keep deepening strategic ties with Washington and did not regard U.S. security support as a concession to be traded with Beijing. (<a href='https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/taiwans-message-to-china-after-trump-xi-meet-us-arms-sales-safe-washington-ties-to-deepen/articleshow/131120331.cms'>Times of India</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/640055-xi-trump-results-summit/'>What did Trump and Xi achieve in Beijing?</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/2000415/trump-concludes-visit-to-china-says-made-fantastic-trade-deals-with-xi'>Trump concludes visit to China, says made &#x27;fantastic trade deals&#x27; with Xi</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/trump-concludes-china-visit-hailing-fantastic-deals/article70983165.ece'>Trump says U.S., China ‘settled a lot of problems’</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/clypj01189lo'>Trump and Xi conclude &#x27;very successful&#x27; talks but few deals confirmed</a> - bbc.com<